  "id": "COMPOUND:1515 - PROTEIN:2687",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) may exert a modulatory effect on Cytochrome c oxidase subunit 6A2, mitochondrial through multiple distinct pathways. Pathway 1 links UDCA to Ribosomal protein S6 kinase 2, which is then targeted by 4-(2-Hydroxyethyl)-5-methyloxazole, a compound that ultimately targets the cytochrome c oxidase subunit 6A2. Pathway 2 connects UDCA to L132, which is targeted by cholic acid, a bile acid that also directs to the same subunit. Pathway 3 shows UDCA targeting P-glycoprotein 1, which is targeted by Stigmastane-3,6-diol, another compound that also directs to the cytochrome c oxidase subunit 6A2. These pathways collectively imply that UDCA may influence mitochondrial function through different molecular mechanisms, potentially affecting oxidative phosphorylation and cellular energy production. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between UDCA and the cytochrome c oxidase subunit 6A2, but the exact nature of the interaction remains unclear, as the specific molecular interactions and regulatory pathways are not fully elucidated. The observed effects may be mediated through various biochemical processes, including bile acid signaling and protein kinase activation, but further experimental validation is required to confirm the precise mechanism.",
